{"agent":"lane-verify","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"`with_no_claim` only adds a `claim` when the no-claim condition is true; it never clears an existing `claim`. If it is called on a lane that already has a claim (e.g. a cached lane view, or any caller that reuses `Lane` objects from a previous `with_no_claim`/`build` call), a later call where `probe` finds a live pid, or where `cfg.verify.no_claim_phrases` is emptied, still returns the old claim. For example, passing `Lane(..., state=S.STOPPED, claim=\"no claim made: old\")` with `probe=lambda item: True` returns a lane whose `claim` remains set, even though the docstring says a live pid means a claim is made.","digest":"1c95387654ec548d","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/lanes/ledger.py:with_no_claim","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/lanes/ledger.py:with_no_claim"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"For a bug/lesson/decision/rule expectation, `_check_other` decides existence only from `rid in table`. The work-item path explicitly treats a tombstone (`it.removed`) as `MISSING`, but the other-kind path does not inspect the table entry. Given a folded state where a deleted bug remains in `st.bugs` as a removed record (the same kind of tombstone the code already handles for `st.items`), a manifest record `{\"id\": \"b1\", \"kind\": \"bug\"}` returns `Verdict(\"b1\", OK)`. The live bug is missing, yet `Result.all_ok` is true and the caller can report success.","digest":"192205c343ee2040","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:_check_other","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:_check_other"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`expectations` iterates `ids` and `tags` directly, so a bare string is treated as an iterable of characters rather than a single id or tag. Given `expectations(\"A1\")`, the function returns expectations for ids `\"A\"` and `\"1\"`, not for `\"A1\"`. If the state contains live items `\"A\"` and `\"1\"`, `check` returns all `OK` and `Result.all_ok` is true even though the intended id `\"A1\"` is missing. The same problem occurs for `tags=\"wave:1\"`, which becomes six one-character tag expectations.","digest":"3b2e3fc8e4b5c339","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:expectations","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:expectations"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`parse` does not validate that `kind` is a string. A manifest record with `kind = [\"bug\"]` (valid TOML/JSON array syntax) passes parsing, then `check` executes `if kind in _OTHER_TABLES:`. Because a list is unhashable, this raises `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'` instead of the intended `ManifestError`/exit-2 manifest failure.","digest":"d23bc6c6adb3a7f0","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:check","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:check"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The command parameter help explicitly says `ids` may be “comma separated in one string, or a list,” but `_call` never splits commas: `ids=[ids] if isinstance(ids, str) else list(ids)`. Given an MCP call with `ids=\"A,B\"` (or a CLI call with one positional argument `\"A,B\"`), the command is invoked with `ids=[\"A,B\"]`, so the state is checked for one record id containing a comma instead of two ids `A` and `B`.","digest":"fb3ee21a22b4aabf","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/verify_filed.py:_call","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/verify_filed.py:_call"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"The documented TOML manifest example uses a top-level `[[record]]` table array, while the same help says a JSON manifest is “an object with `defaults` and `records`” and that an unknown manifest field exits 2. A user who creates the TOML manifest exactly as shown has a top-level key `record`, not `records`. If the parser follows the documented `records` key, this manifest contains zero expected records, so `ddflow verify-filed --manifest ...` can exit 0 vacuously (“every record is OK”) even though none of the claimed ids were checked; if it treats `record` as unknown instead, the documented example itself exits 2. Either way the documented manifest format is wrong and can produce a false OK or a spurious refusal.","digest":"cea2f80be62ebccc","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/verify-filed.md:19","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/verify-filed.md:19"},{"chunk":8,"detail":"The `SEG-inflight-registry` record has a non-empty `needs` list (`needs = [\"RC-tree-result-cache\"]`) but carries `tags = [\"wave:0\", \"segments\"]`. Under the stated expectation formula and rule `r-task-phase-needs-wave`, a task’s wave tag must be `1 + highest wave among its needs`; with any non-negative wave on the prerequisite, the expected tag is at least `wave:1`. For example, if `RC-tree-result-cache` is filed as a task with `wave:0`, this manifest expects `SEG-inflight-registry` with `wave:0`, so a correctly filed `wave:1` record would be reported WRONG, while a filed `wave:0` record would be accepted despite violating the wave rule.","digest":"b4bf486232e8e276","family":"alibaba","location":"docs/ddflow/verify-filed-manifest.toml:29-30","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"docs/ddflow/verify-filed-manifest.toml:29-30"}],"chunks_total":10,"coverage":"10/10 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":45229,"diff_sha":"c6adf24d939ab0e5adc3e24a4ff3a088482df4177b5c36222228203ca19747cb","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/VR-verify-filed","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":1433.2,"ex