r ids `\"A\"` and `\"1\"`, not for `\"A1\"`. If the state contains live items `\"A\"` and `\"1\"`, `check` returns all `OK` and `Result.all_ok` is true even though the intended id `\"A1\"` is missing. The same problem occurs for `tags=\"wave:1\"`, which becomes six one-character tag expectations.","digest":"3b2e3fc8e4b5c339","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:expectations","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:expectations"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`parse` does not validate that `kind` is a string. A manifest record with `kind = [\"bug\"]` (valid TOML/JSON array syntax) passes parsing, then `check` executes `if kind in _OTHER_TABLES:`. Because a list is unhashable, this raises `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'` instead of the intended `ManifestError`/exit-2 manifest failure.","digest":"d23bc6c6adb3a7f0","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:check","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/verify_filed.py:check"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The command parameter help explicitly says `ids` may be “comma separated in one string, or a list,” but `_call` never splits commas: `ids=[ids] if isinstance(ids, str) else list(ids)`. Given an MCP call with `ids=\"A,B\"` (or a CLI call with one positional argument `\"A,B\"`), the command is invoked with `ids=[\"A,B\"]`, so the state is checked for one record id containing a comma instead of two ids `A` and `B`.","digest":"fb3ee21a22b4aabf","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/verify_filed.py:_call","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/verify_filed.py:_call"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"The documented TOML manifest example uses a top-level `[[record]]` table array, while the same help says a JSON manifest is “an object with `defaults` and `records`” and that an unknown manifest field exits 2. A user who creates the TOML manifest exactly as shown has a top-level key `record`, not `records`. If the parser follows the documented `records` key, this manifest contains zero expected records, so `ddflow verify-filed --manifest ...` can exit 0 vacuously (“every record is OK”) even though none of the claimed ids were checked; if it treats `record` as unknown instead, the documented example itself exits 2. Either way the documented manifest format is wrong and can produce a false OK or a spurious refusal.","digest":"cea2f80be62ebccc","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/verify-filed.md:19","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/verify-filed.md:19"},{"chunk":8,"detail":"The `SEG-inflight-registry` record has a non-empty `needs` list (`needs = [\"RC-tree-result-cache\"]`) but carries `tags = [\"wave:0\", \"segments\"]`. Under the stated expectation formula and rule `r-task-phase-needs-wave`, a task’s wave tag must be `1 + highest wave among its needs`; with any non-negative wave on the prerequisite, the expected tag is at least `wave:1`. For example, if `RC-tree-result-cache` is filed as a task with `wave:0`, this manifest expects `SEG-inflight-registry` with `wave:0`, so a correctly filed `wave:1` record would be reported WRONG, while a filed `wave:0` record would be accepted despite violating the wave rule.","digest":"b4bf486232e8e276","family":"alibaba","location":"docs/ddflow/verify-filed-manifest.toml:29-30","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"docs/ddflow/verify-filed-manifest.toml:29-30"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"The new line `lane = replace(lane, claim=\"\")` clears the lane’s claim **before** `_claims_background(st, lane, cfg.verify.no_claim_phrases)` is called. `_claims_background` is the probe that decides whether a stopped lane’s claim claims background work, and it receives both the lane and the configured phrases, so clearing the claim first removes the text it needs to inspect. For example, given a stopped lane with `claim=\"background work still running\"`, `cfg.verify.no_claim_phrases=[\"background\"]`, and no live pid/growing output, the intended result is `claim=\"no claim made: ...\"`. With this change, `_claims_background` sees an empty claim and returns no hit, so `with_no_claim` emits `claim=\"\"` instead of the required no-claim status. The stale-claim fix should compute `hit` from the original lane and clear only when `hit` is false (or clear after the probe), not before it.","digest":"802b21fdafed3a00","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/lanes/ledger.py:with_no_claim","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/lanes/ledger.py:with_no_claim"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_findings":1,"delta_from":"48ea398cb122c8d640e659760a30c0b0d383a96c","delta_raised":["802b21fdafed3a00"],"delta_rounds":1,"diff_chars":7565,"diff_sha":"0fb520cd21b3a7864b9ade0ef6d3f238894f2382004d18592c3a845381981535","diff_source":"delta review of 2 commits since 48ea398cb1","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":389.8,"family":"alibaba","findings":8,"full_coverage":"10/10 chunk(s) reviewed","max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"[REDACTED:name]","output_digest":"ac027dc85bc39c19","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/VR-verify-filed.rubber_duck.[REDACTED:name].20261113T053045Z-902fbff2.jsonl","reason":"","review_degraded":[{"at":1794548235.1041286,"family":"google","gate":"rubber_duck","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","reason":"HTTP 429","reviewer":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite"},{"at":1794548235.2088048,"family":"xai","gate":"rubber_duck","model":"grok-4.7","reason":"HTTP 403","reviewer":"grok-4.7"}],"review_kind":"delta","reviewed":[1,2],"reviewed_head":"fe566169032744606a5efb07e841bac958f1b568","reviewed_paths":{"README.md":"100644 c1d86fe362c6a7a820a886b8c4194a3ca312fdab","ddflow/api/lane_views.py":"100644 c677ebd74f6b